## 3. RedisTemplate Generic Types

Yes! You can use anything, not just `String, Object`.

```java
// Most common - flexible
RedisTemplate<String, Object> template

// String keys, String values
RedisTemplate<String, String> template

// String keys, User object values  
RedisTemplate<String, User> template

// String keys, List values
RedisTemplate<String, List<DeptStats>> template
```

### Which to use when?

```java
// StringRedisTemplate → shortcut for <String, String>
// Use when storing simple strings, numbers, flags
@Autowired
StringRedisTemplate stringTemplate;

stringTemplate.opsForValue().set("count", "42");
stringTemplate.opsForValue().set("flag",  "true");


// RedisTemplate<String, Object> → most flexible
// Use when storing Java objects (User, List, Map etc)
@Autowired
RedisTemplate<String, Object> objectTemplate;

objectTemplate.opsForValue().set("user:1", userObject);
objectTemplate.opsForHash().putAll("session:1", sessionMap);


// RedisTemplate<String, User> → type safe
// Use when ONE specific type only
@Autowired
RedisTemplate<String, User> userTemplate;

userTemplate.opsForValue().set("user:1", user); // no casting needed!
User u = userTemplate.opsForValue().get("user:1"); // no casting!
```

