## 5. Serializer Decides Format

```java
// StringRedisSerializer for keys → stores as plain text
template.setKeySerializer(new StringRedisSerializer());
// Redis key: user:1  ← clean readable

// JdkSerializationRedisSerializer (DEFAULT) → ugly bytes
template.setValueSerializer(new JdkSerializationRedisSerializer());
// Redis value: \xac\xed\x00\x05t\x00...  ← unreadable!

// GenericJackson2JsonRedisSerializer → clean JSON
template.setValueSerializer(new GenericJackson2JsonRedisSerializer());
// Redis value: {"id":1,"name":"John"}  ← readable ✅

// Jackson2JsonRedisSerializer → JSON but for specific type
template.setValueSerializer(new Jackson2JsonRedisSerializer<>(User.class));
// Same as above but only for User type
```

### Difference between two JSON serializers:

```java
// GenericJackson2JsonRedisSerializer
// Stores class name inside JSON
// {"@class":"com.example.User","id":1,"name":"John"}
// ✅ Can deserialize back to correct type automatically
// ❌ Slightly larger storage

// Jackson2JsonRedisSerializer
// Does NOT store class name
// {"id":1,"name":"John"}
// ✅ Smaller storage
// ❌ You must tell it which class to deserialize to
```

